---
description: Keep behavior with its owning module and justify changes to shared contracts and delivery scope.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Module boundaries

Apply to new or materially changed behavior across modules. Follow subsystem
ownership rules; unrelated code need not be reorganized.

- **Choose ownership first.** Identify the owner, existing contract, and missing
  shared capability in the task's existing plan or PR. No separate document or
  approval is required. Convenient local variables do not justify orchestrator logic.
- **Isolate implementation details.** Provider protocols, payload parsing,
  workarounds, and feature sequencing belong with their owner. Shared code consumes
  normalized domain contracts, not provider payloads or SDK types. Small registries
  and dispatch branches may select implementations and delegate behavior.
- **Keep contracts narrow.** Reuse extension points; add only what the workflow
  needs. Pass required dependencies, not the whole service context. Moving code
  or renaming interfaces does not remove coupling. A coherent shared contract
  should survive provider removal or substitution. One consumer can justify it;
  speculative reuse or another fixture alone cannot. Preserve provider differences
  rather than forcing a universal interface.
- **Preserve invariant ownership.** Authorization, company isolation, audit,
  transactions, and resource ownership retain their existing owners. Adapters
  must neither bypass controls nor create parallel enforcement paths.
- **Explain shared changes.** For each substantive change outside the owner,
  explain the capability, why existing contracts are insufficient, affected
  consumers, and behavior with the feature absent or disabled. Group related files.
  Catalog entries, branding, schemas, and registration may stay in established
  locations without teaching shared execution the provider protocol.
- **Separate ownership from delivery scope.** Even well-factored platform work
  may need a separate reviewable slice or explicit PR-stack dependency to clarify
  effects on existing consumers. Keep tests and docs with behavior. Preserve
  requested features and delivery constraints; avoid unusable intermediate states
  and arbitrary PR/file counts.

**Verify:** Exercise observable behavior through new and relevant existing
consumers, including affected absent/disabled states—not just helper calls.
A different-protocol fixture can expose coupling but proves neither production
compatibility nor the abstraction's necessity.

**Review:** Flag misplaced behavior, leaked private representations, duplicated
invariant ownership, and unexplained shared changes; name the proper owner and
contract. Recheck necessity and scope after refactoring. Identifier searches,
line counts, and passing CI alone do not establish sound boundaries.
